      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIMS",
          "content": "This study examined the compliance of French children with physical activity (PA) guidelines, using objective assessments of PA."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The study involved 252 children aged 9.9 +/- 0.9 years, with mean height and weight of 1.39 +/- 0.08 m and 35.8 +/- 8.8 kg, respectively. Their usual PA was evaluated during a week using an Actigraph accelerometer."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The time spent in a moderate-to-vigorous PA (MVPA) ranged from 142 +/- 44 min x d(-1) to 25 +/- 18 min/day according to the cut-offs used. Boys were significantly more active than girls (p < or = .001). Overweight/obese children spent significantly less time in MVPA as determined with cut-off points at 3200 cpm (-26%) or 3600 cpm (-35%) (p < .01). Between 5% (8% of boys vs 1% of girls, p < .0001) and 9% (14% of boys vs 3% of girls, p < .0001) of children probably met the PA guidelines. No relationships were found with socioeconomic status."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "This high proportion of insufficiently active children (> 90%), together with the relatively high proportion of overweight children in this area, advocates a more aggressive PA promotion project, which should target all children whatever their social origins."
        }
      ]},
